WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Fires and five-year-old fine-boasts produced near-zero recruitment among reseeding proteas. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 187. In the early 1970s, managers at Bontabok National Park brought in rotational burning to keep grassland open for the Bontabok, the park had been created to protect. Fred Kruger and other researchers in the Cape Fine-boasts had documented Fires role in maintaining those shrublands, and they supported a shift from fire exclusion to active prescribed burning.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.540
A 1968 policy change had already established compartment-based burning on planned frequencies

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.400
of 12 to 15 years in conservation areas.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.080
Bontabok carried a different local pressure.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.900
Bontabok numbers had fallen under earlier protection regimes, and the Antelope preferred short-lived

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.300
grasses.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.380
A four-year rotation appeared to keep fuel loads low near settlements and to favor that

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:52.920
forage.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:58.420
Early research by PG Jordan had already set a minimum safe interval of eight years for

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.440
proteorepins, yet the grazing objective took precedence in the park.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.620
The decision rested on what was then known about fuel reduction and herbivore habitat,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.260
not on detailed data for every receding shrub, and staff treated the cycle as a practical

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.040
compromise that lowered wildfire risk and improved forage without yet measuring the seed

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.560
accumulation schedules of the slowest maturing proteoids.

00:01:22.459 --> 00:01:27.259
Park staff divided the lowland fine-boasts and renostervelled into burning compartments

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.559
and applied fire on the four-year schedule from the early 1970s.

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:37.699
Records later showed that actual return periods average between 5.8 and 8.0 years

00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:41.179
because of operational constraints and occasional wildfires.

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.039
Proponents noted reduced fuel accumulation and improved visibility for visitors.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:50.779
Skeptics in the emerging fine-boast research community pointed to maturation data

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:53.459
showing that some proteoids needed longer intervals,

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:56.739
but the grazing mandate remained the operational priority.

00:01:56.739 --> 00:02:01.819
The coordinated programs under Fred Krueger continued to gather fire effects data at sites

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:03.059
such as Yonkershoek.

00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:08.099
The uniform rotation was adopted because it delivered measurable control over fuel height,

00:02:08.499 --> 00:02:13.019
and because each compartment ran on the same clock, simple height measurements could predict

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:14.519
and document fuel loads.

00:02:15.427 --> 00:02:20.687
Post-fire surveys at Bonta-Bach recorded the progressive loss of slow maturing seed regenerating

00:02:20.687 --> 00:02:25.367
shrubs, including proteorepins, once the shortened cycle was established.

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:31.327
Studies of eastern coastal fine-boasts in the Tsitsukama and Outaniqua mountains found juvenile

00:02:31.327 --> 00:02:37.627
periods of 4-9 years for Serotonous Proteacy. Recruitment reached replacement levels only after

00:02:37.627 --> 00:02:43.047
fires and vegetation at least 7 years old, and it peaked in stands that had remained unburned

00:02:43.047 --> 00:02:50.307
for 38 years. A 2021 analysis across multiple proteopopulations derived conservative minimum intervals

00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:55.987
levels of 11 to 14 years when the 50% flowering for three seasons rule was applied to the

00:02:55.987 --> 00:02:57.427
slowest maturing stands.

00:02:57.867 --> 00:03:03.087
The 2025 study of long-term records in the out-and-equal-world heritage site confirmed

00:03:03.087 --> 00:03:08.647
that intervals shorter than 12 to 14 years, especially on drier slopes, produced recruitment

00:03:08.647 --> 00:03:09.127
failure.

00:03:09.707 --> 00:03:14.887
The shrub layer stayed inside fuel targets, so managers saw no immediate problem in vegetation

00:03:14.887 --> 00:03:18.687
structure even as the seed bank of the dominant reciders collapsed.

00:03:18.687 --> 00:03:19.567
2.

00:03:19.727 --> 00:03:24.287
Second-order effects at the Junkershook comparison sites included reduced cover and biomass

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:28.747
of longer-lived shrubs and increased dominance by short-lived granminoids.

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:33.967
Third-order effects appeared when that simplified community proved less resilient to subsequent

00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:36.527
droughts and to alien plant incursions.

00:03:36.967 --> 00:03:41.907
A plant that first flowered at year 5 or 6 could contribute seed in only one or two seasons

00:03:41.907 --> 00:03:47.407
before the next fire removed the adults and repeated over several rotations the cumulative seed

00:03:47.407 --> 00:03:51.327
input fell below the threshold needed to replace the pre-fire population.

00:03:51.987 --> 00:03:56.967
Those long unburned stands had accumulated multiple seasons of seed, so even variable

00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:01.027
post-fire conditions still produced replacement level or higher seedling counts.

00:04:01.747 --> 00:04:06.487
The policy optimized the variable it measured, fuel height, while the regeneration niche

00:04:06.487 --> 00:04:09.787
of the obligate reciters sat outside any monitored threshold.

00:04:10.903 --> 00:04:15.563
Later research programs documented the mismatch and recommended raising minimum intervals

00:04:15.563 --> 00:04:16.903
to nine years or more.

00:04:17.603 --> 00:04:22.403
Bontabok managers adjusted burning plans to allow longer rotations in selected units,

00:04:22.783 --> 00:04:26.503
though operational and safety pressures continued to compress some intervals.

00:04:27.143 --> 00:04:31.803
The 2010 review of the Park's regime noted that repeated burning of immature vegetation

00:04:31.803 --> 00:04:34.723
had already removed several slow maturing species.

00:04:35.543 --> 00:04:40.023
Subsequent policies in the broader fine-boast region incorporated site-specific juvenile

00:04:40.023 --> 00:04:45.303
period data, rather than fixed rotations, yet wildfires still dominate actual

00:04:45.303 --> 00:04:51.043
fire occurrence. No single reversal restored the lost proteopopulations where local seed sources

00:04:51.043 --> 00:04:56.203
had been eliminated, and where seed banks had been exhausted, even extended intervals could

00:04:56.203 --> 00:05:01.650
not recreate the lost cohorts without active reintroduction. The actor was the compartment

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:07.570
schedule, which rewarded uniform fuel reduction while the rule for proteopresistant stayed unmeasured.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.410
When rotation length is set by one reading, fuel height or grazing sword,

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:17.490
The regeneration requirements of the slowest maturing species fall outside the Managed Window.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:23.970
Managers who set intervals by one dominant variable can test whether the slowest life history trait in the system

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.750
still completes its cycle under that same interval.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:32.410
Bonta box fuel height compartments are a single gauge rule of the kind environmental intelligence

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.150
weighs against the species a hazard plan never counts.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.810
How many current hazard reduction plans would still hold if the maturation time

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.310
of the most sensitive native species were added as a binding constraint.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:47.370
We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

